window.AP_EUROPEAN_HISTORY_DECKS = [{"id":"apeuro-unit-1","name":"AP European History - Unit 1","shortName":"Unit 1: Renaissance and Exploration","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u1-c1","front":"Italian Renaissance","back":"The Italian Renaissance was the rebirth of interest in classical antiquity (Greco-Roman) that impacted education, culture, and art. Capitalism (not yet free-market) and modern banking techniques began to develop during this time, catapulting the city-state of Florence to cultural and economic prominence.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c2","front":"Francesco Petrarch","back":"Francesco Petrarch was the father of humanism, the main intellectual component of the Renaissance. Humanists believed that human nature and achievements, evident in the classics, were worthy of admiration and contemplation.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c3","front":"humanism","back":"A Renaissance intellectual movement that revived Greek and Roman learning and emphasized human dignity, individual potential, education in the humanities, and active participation in civic life.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c4","front":"civic humanism","back":"A facet of humanism was civic humanism, which encouraged scholars to read ancient Greco-Roman documents that educated them on how to become a better citizen. These documents encouraged democracy.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c5","front":"individualism","back":"Humanism also stressed individualism, the optimism and self-confidence in one’s own achievements and pursuit of knowledge.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c6","front":"Pico della Mirandola","back":"Pico della Mirandola’s Oration on the Dignity of Man (1496) asserted that humans were at the center of divine creation because of their unique gift of free will. His Oration was in line with Renaissance humanism and was the first printed book ever banned by the Catholic Church.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c7","front":"naturalism","back":"An artistic approach that represents people and the physical world with realistic proportions, movement, perspective, and close observation of nature.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c8","front":"patronage","back":"patronage: Wealthy and influential Italians, such as the Medici family, used their wealth to support the art. Commissioned art was used to glorify these families and their cities.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c9","front":"Avignon Schism","back":"The papacy’s extensive patronage of the arts rebuilt the Vatican’s prestige after years of decline after the Avignon Schism, a period in which bishops in both Rome and Avignon claimed to be the true pope.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c10","front":"Niccolò Machiavelli","back":"Niccolò Machiavelli wrote The Prince, which encouraged leaders to learn from the shrewd and ruthless tactics of Roman emperors. He believed that leaders should neither be loved, nor hated, but feared.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c11","front":"Baldassare Castiglione","back":"Baldassare Castiglione wrote The Courtier, which became a manual of proper behavior for upper-class men and women. It also influenced the separate spheres model of gender inequality.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c12","front":"Northern Renaissance","back":"Pieter Bruegel’s Northern Renaissance piece The Harvesters depicts both men and women working in the fields.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c13","front":"Christian humanism","back":"Christian humanism, embodied in the writings of Desiderius Erasmus, employed Renaissance learning in the service of religious reform. Erasmus “laid the egg that Luther hatched,” rejecting both the notion of predestination and the absolute power of the Catholic church.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c14","front":"Desiderius Erasmus","back":"Christian humanism, embodied in the writings of Desiderius Erasmus, employed Renaissance learning in the service of religious reform. Erasmus “laid the egg that Luther hatched,” rejecting both the notion of predestination and the absolute power of the Catholic church.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c15","front":"Ferdinand and Isabella","back":"Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castile were the monarchs who united dynastic Spain, strengthened royal authority over nobles and clergy, completed the conquest of Granada, enforced Catholic religious unity through the Spanish Inquisition, and sponsored Columbus's 1492 voyage.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c16","front":"new monarchs","back":"Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castille, the king and queen of Spain in 1480, were “new monarchs,” meaning that they limited the power of the nobility and the clergy in order to centralize their power. They agreed to make Catholicism the national religion of Spain in return for the Pope’s allowance of them to appoint every church official in their country.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c17","front":"Black Death","back":"Anti-Semitism had already become worse in Europe after the Black Death, and Ferdinand and Isabella chose to target Jews in order to centralize their power. In the Spanish Inquisition, they ordered Spain’s Jewish population to either convert to Catholicism (and become “conversos”) or leave the country.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c18","front":"Spanish Inquisition","back":"In the Spanish Inquisition, they ordered Spain’s Jewish population to either convert to Catholicism (and become “conversos”) or leave the country. Under suspicion that many conversos had not truly become Catholic, the monarchs eventually forced as much of the Jewish (and Muslim) population as they could to leave entirely.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c19","front":"conversos","back":"In the Spanish Inquisition, they ordered Spain’s Jewish population to either convert to Catholicism (and become “conversos”) or leave the country. Under suspicion that many conversos had not truly become Catholic, the monarchs eventually forced as much of the Jewish (and Muslim) population as they could to leave entirely.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c20","front":"Treaty of Tordesillas","back":"In the 1492 Treaty of Tordesillas, Spain and Portugal made an agreement through the Catholic Church over who can claim which areas in the New World. Spain had the right to the (at the time undiscovered by Europeans) areas west of the imaginary line, and Portugal had the lands to the east.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c21","front":"Henry the Navigator","back":"During this time, Portuguese Prince Henry the Navigator funded and encouraged exploration. He sponsored numerous West African expeditions.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c22","front":"Christopher Columbus","back":"Christopher Columbus pioneered the Spanish domination of South America; Spain conquered the rather advanced Aztec and Inca empires due to the more advanced European weaponry. However, the largest factor contributing to European success was the Columbian Exchange.","sourcePage":2},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c23","front":"Columbian Exchange","back":"The transfer of plants, animals, diseases, people, and ideas between the Americas and Afro-Eurasia after 1492. It transformed diets and economies, devastated Indigenous American populations through disease, and helped expand European colonization and Atlantic slavery.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c24","front":"Atlantic slave trade","back":"This brought about the Atlantic slave trade, in which enslaved peoples were bought in Africa and sent to American plantations, in which the fruits of their labor was sent to Europe.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u1-c25","front":"printing press","back":"The development of the printing press resulted in the mass production of classical texts, weakening the Catholic Church’s control over information and promoting secularism. This resulted in the Church having less control over intellectual life.","sourcePage":3}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-1-f1","prompt":"Italian Renaissance"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-1-f2","prompt":"Renaissance humanism"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-2","name":"AP European History - Unit 2","shortName":"Unit 2: Age of Reformation","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u2-c1","front":"Martin Luther","back":"Martin Luther, the most famous figure of the Protestant Reformation and founder of Lutheranism and Protestantism, believed that salvation is initiated by God (not the Church), authority is rested in the Bible alone, and that the Church should not be a hierarchical clerical institution.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c2","front":"John Calvin","back":"John Calvin was a believer in predestination, the concept that men cannot actively work to achieve salvation; God already decided who would be saved and who would be damned.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c3","front":"Protestant Reformation","back":"Protestant leaders Martin Luther and John Calvin called for separation from the Catholic Church, bringing about the Protestant Reformation.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c4","front":"Lutheranism","back":"Martin Luther, the most famous figure of the Protestant Reformation and founder of Lutheranism and Protestantism, believed that salvation is initiated by God (not the Church), authority is rested in the Bible alone, and that the Church should not be a hierarchical clerical institution.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c5","front":"Ulrich Zwingli","back":"A Swiss Protestant reformer who led the Reformation in Zürich, rejected many Catholic rituals and sacraments, and argued that scripture should be the sole foundation of Christian practice.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c6","front":"predestination","back":"John Calvin was a believer in predestination, the concept that men cannot actively work to achieve salvation; God already decided who would be saved and who would be damned.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c7","front":"Holy Roman Empire","back":"The decentralized states of the Holy Roman Empire allowed for reform movements to gain traction, however, Habsburg Holy Roman Emperor Charles V had ambitions for an entirely Catholic empire. After much conflict between Catholics and Protestants and the Augsburg Confession from the German Protestant princes, Charles V and the princes came to an agreement called the Peace of Augsburg in 1555.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c8","front":"Habsburg","back":"The German princes were given independence from the HRE by the Habsburgs.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c9","front":"Charles V","back":"The decentralized states of the Holy Roman Empire allowed for reform movements to gain traction, however, Habsburg Holy Roman Emperor Charles V had ambitions for an entirely Catholic empire. After much conflict between Catholics and Protestants and the Augsburg Confession from the German Protestant princes, Charles V and the princes came to an agreement called the Peace of Augsburg in 1555.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c10","front":"Augsburg Confession","back":"After much conflict between Catholics and Protestants and the Augsburg Confession from the German Protestant princes, Charles V and the princes came to an agreement called the Peace of Augsburg in 1555.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c11","front":"German Protestant princes","back":"After much conflict between Catholics and Protestants and the Augsburg Confession from the German Protestant princes, Charles V and the princes came to an agreement called the Peace of Augsburg in 1555.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c12","front":"Peace of Augsburg","back":"Renounced the Edict of Restitution (which had renounced the Peace of Augsburg) and expanded the Peace of Augsburg to include Calvinists in addition to Catholics and Lutherans. Once again the rulers of each state could determine its religion.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c13","front":"Calvinism","back":"These Protestant ideas (specifically Lutheranism and Calvinism) brought about religious change in central, northern, and eastern Europe. Protestant ideas were appealing to peasants and city governments alike, and the development of Protestantism as well as English King Henry VIII’s formation of the Church of England caused more and more people to depart from Catholicism.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c14","front":"King Henry VIII","back":"Protestant ideas were appealing to peasants and city governments alike, and the development of Protestantism as well as English King Henry VIII’s formation of the Church of England caused more and more people to depart from Catholicism.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c15","front":"Catholic Reformation","back":"In an attempt to purify its image and take back supporters, the Catholic Church launched its Catholic Reformation (also known as the Counter-Reformation). The church drove for internal reform, removing corrupt policies and establishing new religious orders such as the Jesuits.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c16","front":"Counter-Reformation","back":"In an attempt to purify its image and take back supporters, the Catholic Church launched its Catholic Reformation (also known as the Counter-Reformation). The church drove for internal reform, removing corrupt policies and establishing new religious orders such as the Jesuits.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c17","front":"Jesuits","back":"The church drove for internal reform, removing corrupt policies and establishing new religious orders such as the Jesuits.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c18","front":"Council of Trent","back":"Pope Paul III’s mid-16th century Council of Trent addressed what reforms had to be made in the Church. The Council laid a solid basis for the spiritual renewal of the Catholic Church and its faith, organization, and practice.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c19","front":"mannerism","back":"The mannerism (twisting proportions) and baroque (dark backgrounds; high contrast) styles of art were grand, emotional, and visually interesting Catholic propaganda used to reclaim Catholic support.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c20","front":"baroque","back":"The mannerism (twisting proportions) and baroque (dark backgrounds; high contrast) styles of art were grand, emotional, and visually interesting Catholic propaganda used to reclaim Catholic support.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c21","front":"witchcraft","back":"Yet, despite their disagreements, both Catholics and Protestants saw pagans as agents of Satan, both ruthlessly trying and executing thousands of women accused of witchcraft.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c22","front":"Francis I","back":"So, the Concordat of Bologna had King Francis I agree to recognize the supremacy of the papacy over a universal council. In return, the French crown gained the right to appoint all French bishops, which allowed for economic growth for the French crown.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c23","front":"iconoclasm","back":"Strong religious fervor combined with a weak French monarchy led to civil violence between Catholic royalist lords and Calvinist anti-monarchical lords, all while ordinary men and women demonstrated iconoclasm by destroying religious images.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c24","front":"Saint Bartholomew’s Day Massacre","back":"At the Saint Bartholomew’s Day Massacre in 1572, thousands of Protestants were killed by Catholic mobs who thought they were doing God’s and the king’s will. This massacre took place at the Protestant Henry of Navarre’s wedding.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c25","front":"politique","back":"Other politiques include the Protestant Queen Elizabeth I of England and the Catholic King Philip II of Spain. These two clashed in 1588, in which Elizabeth destroyed Spain’s Spanish Armada.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c26","front":"Spanish Armada","back":"These two clashed in 1588, in which Elizabeth destroyed Spain’s Spanish Armada.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c27","front":"defenestration of Prague","back":"Started with the defenestration of Prague in 1618, when Calvinist rebels threw Bohemian royal council members out of a window (though they survived). Holy Roman Emperor Ferdinand II got the support of king Maximilian I of Bavaria to invade Bohemia.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c28","front":"Edict of Restitution","back":"Renounced the Edict of Restitution (which had renounced the Peace of Augsburg) and expanded the Peace of Augsburg to include Calvinists in addition to Catholics and Lutherans. Once again the rulers of each state could determine its religion.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c29","front":"Habsburgs","back":"The German princes were given independence from the HRE by the Habsburgs.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c30","front":"Gustavus Adolphus","back":"However, Swedish king Gustavus Adolphus died in warfare. Wallenstein was assassinated after being caught secretly working with Sweden and France.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c31","front":"Treaty of Prague","back":"The Treaty of Prague was enacted in 1635 after the deaths of both Adolphus and Wallenstein. It strengthened Habsburgs and weakened the German Princes.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c32","front":"Philip IV","back":"Cardinal Richelieu, the chief minister of child-king Louis XIII, wanted to reclaim Alsace and weaken the power of Spain and its Habsburg king Philip IV. He sent large forces to Germany after succeeding against Spain, tipping the balance in the Protestant’s favor.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c33","front":"Peace of Westphalia","back":"The 1648 series of treaties that ended the Thirty Years' War, recognized greater sovereignty for the states of the Holy Roman Empire, and confirmed a broader legal place for Catholic, Lutheran, and Calvinist rulers.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c34","front":"Thirty Years’ War","back":"The Peace of Westphalia was a series of treaties that concluded the Thirty Years’ War.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u2-c35","front":"propaganda","back":"The mannerism (twisting proportions) and baroque (dark backgrounds; high contrast) styles of art were grand, emotional, and visually interesting Catholic propaganda used to reclaim Catholic support.","sourcePage":12}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-2-f1","prompt":"Protestant Reformation"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-2-f2","prompt":"Catholic Reformation"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-2-f3","prompt":"French Wars of Religion"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-2-f4","prompt":"Thirty Years' War"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-3","name":"AP European History - Unit 3","shortName":"Unit 3: Absolutism and Constitutionalism","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u3-c1","front":"Church of England","back":"He wanted religious uniformity (everyone has to belong to the Church of England). In opposition to this were the Calvinist groups Puritans and Separatists.","sourcePage":3},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c2","front":"Henry of Navarre","back":"Henry IV (past Henry of Navarre; r. 1589-1610) defused religious tensions and rebuilt France’s economy after inheriting a France wrecked from religious wars. He founded the Bourbon dynasty.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c3","front":"Huguenot","back":"His Edict of Nantes (1589) allowed Huguenots (French Protestants) the right to worship in 150 traditionally Protestant towns throughout France.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c4","front":"Henry IV","back":"Henry IV (past Henry of Navarre; r. 1589-1610) defused religious tensions and rebuilt France’s economy after inheriting a France wrecked from religious wars. He founded the Bourbon dynasty.","sourcePage":4},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c5","front":"Cardinal Richelieu","back":"Cardinal Richelieu became the first minister of France on behalf of Henry’s young son, Louis XIII.","sourcePage":5},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c6","front":"Peter the Great","back":"In Russia, Peter the Great built up the state, expanded his territory, and Westernized Russia.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c7","front":"Edict of Nantes","back":"Louis revoked king Henry IV’s Edict of Nantes with his own Edict of Fountainbleu in 1685, causing 200,000 Protestants to flee from France.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c8","front":"Fronde","back":"The Fronde occurred in 1648-1653 as a result of the failure of Cardinal Jules Mazarin, first minister of Louis XIII’s son, Louis XIV, and wealthiest man in French history, to meet the costs of the Thirty Years’ War. During these uprisings, the nobles of the robe (members of the Parlement of Paris) encouraged violent protests by commoners, since Mazarin was going to forgo their pay for 4 years.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c9","front":"Cardinal Jules Mazarin","back":"The Fronde occurred in 1648-1653 as a result of the failure of Cardinal Jules Mazarin, first minister of Louis XIII’s son, Louis XIV, and wealthiest man in French history, to meet the costs of the Thirty Years’ War. During these uprisings, the nobles of the robe (members of the Parlement of Paris) encouraged violent protests by commoners, since Mazarin was going to forgo their pay for 4 years.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c10","front":"Louis XIV","back":"Louis XIV, the “Sun King,” based his authority on the concept that kings were God’s rulers on Earth. He formed national policies on war, taxation, law, and religion and removed most of the nobles of the sword (traditional warrior families) from power.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c11","front":"nobles of the robe","back":"During these uprisings, the nobles of the robe (members of the Parlement of Paris) encouraged violent protests by commoners, since Mazarin was going to forgo their pay for 4 years.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c12","front":"Parlement of Paris","back":"During these uprisings, the nobles of the robe (members of the Parlement of Paris) encouraged violent protests by commoners, since Mazarin was going to forgo their pay for 4 years.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c13","front":"nobles of the sword","back":"He formed national policies on war, taxation, law, and religion and removed most of the nobles of the sword (traditional warrior families) from power.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c14","front":"Versailles","back":"Rococo was a fantastical noble-oriented style of art displayed in salons and at the palace of Versailles.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c15","front":"Edict of Fountainbleu","back":"Louis revoked king Henry IV’s Edict of Nantes with his own Edict of Fountainbleu in 1685, causing 200,000 Protestants to flee from France.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c16","front":"Jean-Baptiste Colbert","back":"Jean-Baptiste Colbert was Louis’s controller general from 1665 to his death in 1683. He applied mercantilist policies to France.","sourcePage":6},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c17","front":"mercantilism","back":"mercantilism: A system of economic regulations aimed at increasing the power of the state based on the belief that a nation’s international power was based on its wealth, specifically its supply of gold and silver.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c18","front":"War of Spanish Succession","back":"The War of Spanish Succession (1701-1713) was caused by Louis XIV’s unwillingness to abide by a previous agreement that divided Spanish possessions between France and the Holy Roman Emperor upon the death of the heirless Spanish king Charles II. The Peace of Utrecht ended the war, allowing Louis’s Bourbon grandson Philip to remain king of Spain on the understanding that the French and Spanish crowns would never be united.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c19","front":"Charles II","back":"Charles II makes the Treaty of Dover with his ally Louis XIV, which has England engage in the Franco-Dutch war for financial compensation.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c20","front":"Peace of Utrecht","back":"The Peace of Utrecht ended the war, allowing Louis’s Bourbon grandson Philip to remain king of Spain on the understanding that the French and Spanish crowns would never be united.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c21","front":"Rococo","back":"Rococo was a fantastical noble-oriented style of art displayed in salons and at the palace of Versailles.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c22","front":"salons","back":"Rococo was a fantastical noble-oriented style of art displayed in salons and at the palace of Versailles.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c23","front":"English Civil War","back":"In the English Civil War, the Cavaliers supported the king, while the Roundheads (which included Oliver Cromwell) supported Parliament.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c24","front":"James I","back":"James II was an open Papist (Catholic). He dismissed his Lord of the Treasury after he refused to convert to Catholicism.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c25","front":"Puritans","back":"In opposition to this were the Calvinist groups Puritans and Separatists. They wanted to “purify” and “separate from” the Catholic Church respectively.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c26","front":"Separatists","back":"In opposition to this were the Calvinist groups Puritans and Separatists. They wanted to “purify” and “separate from” the Catholic Church respectively.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c27","front":"Charles I","back":"Charles II makes the Treaty of Dover with his ally Louis XIV, which has England engage in the Franco-Dutch war for financial compensation.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c28","front":"Petition of Right","back":"Parliament responded with the 1628 Petition of Right in response to Charles’s abuse of power. He accepted this, but went through a period of “personal rule” in which he did not call parliament for over a decade.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c29","front":"Cavaliers","back":"In the English Civil War, the Cavaliers supported the king, while the Roundheads (which included Oliver Cromwell) supported Parliament.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c30","front":"Roundheads","back":"In the English Civil War, the Cavaliers supported the king, while the Roundheads (which included Oliver Cromwell) supported Parliament.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c31","front":"Oliver Cromwell","back":"In the English Civil War, the Cavaliers supported the king, while the Roundheads (which included Oliver Cromwell) supported Parliament.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c32","front":"Protectorate","back":"Charles I was beheaded in 1649 and Oliver Cromwell established the Protectorate, a strict Puritanical military dictatorship. Cromwell expelled the Rump Parliament in 1648, leaving only his supporters behind.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c33","front":"Rump Parliament","back":"Cromwell expelled the Rump Parliament in 1648, leaving only his supporters behind. His government was tolerant of Protestants and de-Catholicized the Anglican Church.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c34","front":"Declaration of Indulgence","back":"Charles II issued the Declaration of Indulgence, the non-enforcement of laws against Catholics in England. Parliament, seeing that Charles was becoming a bit too tolerant, issued the Test Acts, which required all office holders to be Anglican.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c35","front":"Test Acts","back":"Parliament, seeing that Charles was becoming a bit too tolerant, issued the Test Acts, which required all office holders to be Anglican.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c36","front":"Treaty of Dover","back":"Charles II makes the Treaty of Dover with his ally Louis XIV, which has England engage in the Franco-Dutch war for financial compensation.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c37","front":"James II","back":"James II was an open Papist (Catholic). He dismissed his Lord of the Treasury after he refused to convert to Catholicism.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c38","front":"William of Orange","back":"William of Orange was invited to rule alongside Mary Stuart, James II’s daughter. They governed England in a joint monarchy and signed the English Bill of Rights, establishing the first constitutional monarchy.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c39","front":"Mary Stuart","back":"William of Orange was invited to rule alongside Mary Stuart, James II’s daughter. They governed England in a joint monarchy and signed the English Bill of Rights, establishing the first constitutional monarchy.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c40","front":"English Bill of Rights","back":"They governed England in a joint monarchy and signed the English Bill of Rights, establishing the first constitutional monarchy.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u3-c41","front":"constitutional monarchy","back":"They governed England in a joint monarchy and signed the English Bill of Rights, establishing the first constitutional monarchy.","sourcePage":8}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-3-f1","prompt":"European absolutism"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-3-f2","prompt":"Louis XIV and French state building"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-3-f3","prompt":"Mercantilism"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-3-f4","prompt":"English Civil War"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-4","name":"AP European History - Unit 4","shortName":"Unit 4: Scientific, Philosophical, and Political Developments","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u4-c1","front":"secularism","back":"Secularism was a concept popularized during the Enlightenment. It argued that government and other institutions should exist entirely separate from religion and the Catholic Church.","sourcePage":1},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c2","front":"divine right of kings","back":"The doctrine that monarchs received their authority directly from God and therefore were accountable to God rather than to representative institutions or their subjects.","sourcePage":7},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c3","front":"Nicolaus Copernicus","back":"Nicolaus Copernicus developed the idea of heliocentrism: the Sun, rather than the Earth, was the center of the universe, and the stars and planets revolved around it. Copernicus published his theories in On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres in 1543.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c4","front":"heliocentrism","back":"Nicolaus Copernicus developed the idea of heliocentrism: the Sun, rather than the Earth, was the center of the universe, and the stars and planets revolved around it. Copernicus published his theories in On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres in 1543.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c5","front":"Johannes Kepler","back":"Johannes Kepler developed the 3 laws of planetary motion: the orbits of the planets around the sun are elliptical (rather than circular), planets do not move at a uniform speed in their orbits, and the time a planet takes to make its complete orbit is precisely related to its distance from the Sun.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c6","front":"3 laws of planetary motion","back":"Johannes Kepler developed the 3 laws of planetary motion: the orbits of the planets around the sun are elliptical (rather than circular), planets do not move at a uniform speed in their orbits, and the time a planet takes to make its complete orbit is precisely related to its distance from the Sun.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c7","front":"Galileo Galilei","back":"Galileo Galilei developed the law of inertia: motion, not rest, is the natural state of an object, and that an object continues in motion forever unless stopped by some external force.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c8","front":"law of inertia","back":"Galileo Galilei developed the law of inertia: motion, not rest, is the natural state of an object, and that an object continues in motion forever unless stopped by some external force.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c9","front":"Isaac Newton","back":"Isaac Newton developed the law of universal gravitation: all objects are attracted to one another and the force of attraction is proportional to the object’s quantity of matter and is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. Newton explained the ideas of his predecessors in his Principia Mathematica, published in 1687.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c10","front":"law of universal gravitation","back":"Isaac Newton developed the law of universal gravitation: all objects are attracted to one another and the force of attraction is proportional to the object’s quantity of matter and is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. Newton explained the ideas of his predecessors in his Principia Mathematica, published in 1687.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c11","front":"the Enlightenment","back":"The Enlightened despots were authoritarian leaders who used their political power according to the principles of the Enlightenment.","sourcePage":8},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c12","front":"philosophes","back":"Diderot: Co-author of the Encyclopedia, the combined manifesto of the French philosophes.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c13","front":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","back":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau: wrote The Social Contract, which argued for equal rights (excluding women and nonwhite races) and that government policy should reflect the general will of the people. Rousseau believed that man needs a strong absolutist centralized power and he attacked private property.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c14","front":"Montesquieu","back":"Montesquieu believed in separation of political powers in government. He believed there were only 3 types of government (monarchy, republicanism, and despotism).","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c15","front":"René Descartes","back":"René Descartes was a champion of rationalism, a secular, critical way of thinking in which nothing was to be accepted on faith and everything was to be submitted to reason. His Cartesian dualism and rationalism is summarized by his most famous quote “I think, therefore I am.”","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c16","front":"rationalism","back":"René Descartes was a champion of rationalism, a secular, critical way of thinking in which nothing was to be accepted on faith and everything was to be submitted to reason. His Cartesian dualism and rationalism is summarized by his most famous quote “I think, therefore I am.”","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c17","front":"Voltaire","back":"Voltaire: Religious toleration and contempt for the power of the Catholic Church.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c18","front":"Diderot","back":"Diderot: Co-author of the Encyclopedia, the combined manifesto of the French philosophes.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c19","front":"John Locke","back":"John Locke wrote The Social Contract, which states that the government is created to protect its citizens’ natural rights of life, liberty, property. He believed that the “divine right of kings” was illegitimate.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c20","front":"Thomas Hobbes","back":"Thomas Hobbes wrote Leviathan to argue that only an absolutist government is able to save man from his natural state of savagery.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c21","front":"Scientific Method","back":"A systematic approach to knowledge based on observation, hypothesis, experimentation, measurement, and revision of conclusions in light of evidence, combining inductive and deductive reasoning.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c22","front":"deductive reasoning","back":"Reasoning that begins with general principles or premises and applies logic to reach a specific conclusion; René Descartes made it central to his rationalist method.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c23","front":"inductive reasoning","back":"Reasoning that builds general conclusions from repeated observations and experiments; Francis Bacon promoted it as a foundation of the scientific method.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c24","front":"Mary Wollstonecraft","back":"An English writer and early advocate of women's equality. In A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, she argued that women appeared inferior because they were denied education and should receive the same rational education as men.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c25","front":"utilitarianism","back":"utilitarianism: the idea of Jeremy Bentham that social policies should promote the “greatest good for the greatest number.”","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c26","front":"Jeremy Bentham","back":"utilitarianism: the idea of Jeremy Bentham that social policies should promote the “greatest good for the greatest number.”","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c27","front":"neoclassical art","back":"neoclassical art: return to Greco-Roman romanticization, such as in Jacques-Louis David’s Oath of the Horatii.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c28","front":"deism","back":"deism: the belief in a distant God but denial of organized religion, basing one’s belief on the light of reason.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c29","front":"Enlightened despots","back":"The Enlightened despots were authoritarian leaders who used their political power according to the principles of the Enlightenment.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c30","front":"War of Austrian Succession","back":"The War of Austrian Succession (1740-1748) began when Frederick the Great of Prussia seized Silesia from Austria’s Maria Theresa. The war’s inconclusive end helped set the stage for the Seven Years’ War.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c31","front":"Frederick the Great","back":"The War of Austrian Succession (1740-1748) began when Frederick the Great of Prussia seized Silesia from Austria’s Maria Theresa. The war’s inconclusive end helped set the stage for the Seven Years’ War.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c32","front":"Maria Theresa","back":"The War of Austrian Succession (1740-1748) began when Frederick the Great of Prussia seized Silesia from Austria’s Maria Theresa. The war’s inconclusive end helped set the stage for the Seven Years’ War.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c33","front":"Seven Years’ War","back":"The war’s inconclusive end helped set the stage for the Seven Years’ War.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c34","front":"Joseph II","back":"The Holy Roman Emperor Joseph II was extremely tolerant, even of Jewish worship. However, at this time, the position of Holy Roman Emperor lacked real power, and the multi-ethnic population of the Empire made it hard for Joseph II to centralize.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u4-c35","front":"Catherine the Great","back":"Catherine the Great of Russia significantly expanded Russian territory. She was a champion of the arts and Enlightenment ideals, though she still practiced serfdom and authoritarianism.","sourcePage":10}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-4-f1","prompt":"Scientific Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-4-f2","prompt":"Enlightenment"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-4-f3","prompt":"Enlightenment political thought"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-4-f4","prompt":"Consumer Revolution"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-5","name":"AP European History - Unit 5","shortName":"Unit 5: Conflict, Crisis, and Reaction in the Late 18th Century","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u5-c1","front":"general will","back":"The Radical phase’s main influence was Jean-Jacques Rousseau (general will). The National Convention and Committee of Public Safety were governing during this period.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c2","front":"natural rights","back":"The Marquis de Lafayette, who fought in the American Revolution, wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Man, which championed equality and other natural rights seen in the American Declaration of Independence.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c3","front":"Jacques-Louis David","back":"Jacques-Louis David’s Napoleon Crossing the Alps is a neoclassical piece glorifying Napoleon’s conquests. It is not to be confused with Paul Delaroche’s Bonaparte Crossing the Alps, which depicts them more realistically.","sourcePage":9},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c4","front":"National Assembly","back":"Louis XVI was forced to call the Estates General, but its failure forced him to allow the National Assembly to meet in 1789.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c5","front":"Legislative Assembly","back":"The National Assembly and Legislative Assembly were governing during this period.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c6","front":"Ancien Regime","back":"France’s loss in the Seven Years’ War and investment in the American Revolution caused a financial crisis for the Ancien Regime. The royal family, Marie Antoinette and Louis XVI, lived in the luxurious palace of Versailles and the First (clergy) and Second (aristocracy) Estates were exempt from taxation while the Third (common masses) Estate starved.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c7","front":"Marie Antoinette","back":"The royal family, Marie Antoinette and Louis XVI, lived in the luxurious palace of Versailles and the First (clergy) and Second (aristocracy) Estates were exempt from taxation while the Third (common masses) Estate starved. However, wealthy bourgeoisie members of the Third Estate were more focused on gaining political power and a liberal constitution than bread.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c8","front":"Louis XVI","back":"Louis XVI was forced to call the Estates General, but its failure forced him to allow the National Assembly to meet in 1789.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c9","front":"Emmanuel Joseph Sièyes","back":"Emmanuel Joseph Sièyes’s What is the Third Estate? (Answer: Everything) describes the Third Estate's oppression pre-Revolution.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c10","front":"Estates General","back":"Louis XVI was forced to call the Estates General, but its failure forced him to allow the National Assembly to meet in 1789.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c11","front":"Marquis de Lafayette","back":"The Marquis de Lafayette, who fought in the American Revolution, wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Man, which championed equality and other natural rights seen in the American Declaration of Independence.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c12","front":"Olympe de Gouges","back":"Olympe de Gouges wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Woman to attack Rousseau’s homemaker view of women.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c13","front":"Fall of the Bastille","back":"The Fall of the Bastille, demonstrating the Great Fear mentality of the French peasantry, was sparked by the starvation and economic concerns of the Third Estate. A similar event was the Women’s March to Versailles.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c14","front":"Great Fear","back":"The Fall of the Bastille, demonstrating the Great Fear mentality of the French peasantry, was sparked by the starvation and economic concerns of the Third Estate. A similar event was the Women’s March to Versailles.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c15","front":"Women’s March to Versailles","back":"An October 1789 march in which thousands of Parisian women, angered by bread shortages and high prices, compelled Louis XVI and the royal family to move from Versailles to Paris, placing them under closer revolutionary control.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c16","front":"Tennis Court Oath","back":"After taking the symbolic Tennis Court Oath, in which the legislative representatives of the National Assembly promised to ratify a constitution, the First French Constitution was ratified in 1791, giving all lawmaking power to the National Assembly.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c17","front":"First French Constitution","back":"After taking the symbolic Tennis Court Oath, in which the legislative representatives of the National Assembly promised to ratify a constitution, the First French Constitution was ratified in 1791, giving all lawmaking power to the National Assembly.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c18","front":"Montagnards","back":"Political Spectrum of the National Assembly (left to right): Montagnards (socialist-leaning), sans-culottes (emerging middle class), Georges Danton’s Jacobins (liberal), Centrists, Girondists (center-right), Monarchiens (monarchist)","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c19","front":"sans-culottes","back":"Political Spectrum of the National Assembly (left to right): Montagnards (socialist-leaning), sans-culottes (emerging middle class), Georges Danton’s Jacobins (liberal), Centrists, Girondists (center-right), Monarchiens (monarchist)","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c20","front":"Jacobins","back":"Political Spectrum of the National Assembly (left to right): Montagnards (socialist-leaning), sans-culottes (emerging middle class), Georges Danton’s Jacobins (liberal), Centrists, Girondists (center-right), Monarchiens (monarchist)","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c21","front":"Girondists","back":"Political Spectrum of the National Assembly (left to right): Montagnards (socialist-leaning), sans-culottes (emerging middle class), Georges Danton’s Jacobins (liberal), Centrists, Girondists (center-right), Monarchiens (monarchist)","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c22","front":"National Convention","back":"The National Convention and Committee of Public Safety were governing during this period.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c23","front":"Committee of Public Safety","back":"The National Convention and Committee of Public Safety were governing during this period.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c24","front":"Maxamilien Robespierre","back":"After the execution of Louis XVI, the radical Jacobin republic led by Maxamilien Robespierre responded to opposition at home and war abroad by instituting the Reign of Terror, fixing prices and wages and pursuing a policy of de-Christianization. Robespierrre formed the Committee of Public Safety, which held dictatorial power and was allowed to use whatever force necessary to defend the Revolution.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c25","front":"Reign of Terror","back":"The Thermidorian Reaction occurred after the violence of the Reign of Terror in 1794, resulting in the execution of Robespierre and the loosening of economic controls. The middle-class, who had led the liberal Revolution of 1789, reasserted their authority.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c26","front":"Constitution of 1793","back":"The liberal Constitution of 1793 was indefinitely suspended in favor of a “revolutionary government.” Presented as a necessary measure to save the republic, the Terror was a weapon directed against all suspected of opposing the revolutionary government.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c27","front":"Thermidorian Reaction","back":"The Thermidorian Reaction occurred after the violence of the Reign of Terror in 1794, resulting in the execution of Robespierre and the loosening of economic controls. The middle-class, who had led the liberal Revolution of 1789, reasserted their authority.","sourcePage":11},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c28","front":"Napoleon Bonaparte","back":"Napoleon Bonaparte overthrew the Directory in 1799 in a coup d’état. From 1799 to 1814, Napoleon Bonaparte was the emperor of France.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c29","front":"Concordat of 1801","back":"Napoleon’s Concordat of 1801 restored Catholic worship in France. It was extremely popular among the French people.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c30","front":"Napoleonic Code","back":"His dictatorial reign employed secret police and censorship, but it was also liberal at times, like in the Napoleonic Code. This begs the question: did Napoleon betray the French Revolution, or did he complete it?","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c31","front":"Continental System","back":"After 1806 Napoleon attempted to create the Continental System, which was meant to halt all trade between Britain and continental Europe. If it had succeeded, it would have destroyed the British economy and its military force.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c32","front":"Treaties of Tilsit","back":"In the 1807 Treaties of Tilsit, Alexander I of Russia pledged his support for Napoleon and his Grand Empire.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c33","front":"Alexander I of Russia","back":"In the 1807 Treaties of Tilsit, Alexander I of Russia pledged his support for Napoleon and his Grand Empire.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c34","front":"Spanish Ulcer","back":"Napoleon’s “Spanish Ulcer” was displayed in Francisco Goya’s Romantic piece The Second of May.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c35","front":"Francisco Goya","back":"Napoleon’s “Spanish Ulcer” was displayed in Francisco Goya’s Romantic piece The Second of May.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c36","front":"invasion of Russia","back":"Napoleon’s invasion of Russia began in June 1812. Napoleon recklessly pressed on toward Moscow even when winter started.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c37","front":"Treaty of Chaumont","back":"Austria and Prussia abandoned Napoleon and joined Russia and Great Britain in the Treaty of Chaumont in 1814. Together, these powers formed the Quadruple Alliance and defeated the French emperor.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c38","front":"Quadruple Alliance","back":"The Quadruple Alliance (Britain, Russia, Prussia, Austria) defeated Napoleon and led the “Concert of Europe,” which sought to uphold peace through conservative policies.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c39","front":"Louis XVIII","back":"Napoleon took back power from Louis XVIII in February 1815. After the frantic period of the Hundred Days, the allies crushed his forces at Waterloo in June.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c40","front":"Constitutional Charter","back":"The new monarch’s Constitutional Charter accepted many of France’s revolutionary changes and kept the guaranteed civil liberties that Napoleon established.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c41","front":"Hundred Days","back":"After the frantic period of the Hundred Days, the allies crushed his forces at Waterloo in June. Napoleon was imprisoned in St.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c42","front":"Metternich","back":"Metternich: Austrian foreign minister and conservative. He wanted stability within and between states, traditional institutions and aristocracy.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c43","front":"Holy Alliance","back":"The Holy Alliance (Austria, Prussia, Russia), a symbol of repression of liberal/revolutionary movements, issued the Karlsbad Decrees in 1819 to repress the German Confederation. These Decrees required the German states to outlaw liberal political organizations, police their universities and newspapers, and establish a permanent committee with spies and informers to clamp down on liberal or radical reformers.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c44","front":"Karlsbad Decrees","back":"The Holy Alliance (Austria, Prussia, Russia), a symbol of repression of liberal/revolutionary movements, issued the Karlsbad Decrees in 1819 to repress the German Confederation. These Decrees required the German states to outlaw liberal political organizations, police their universities and newspapers, and establish a permanent committee with spies and informers to clamp down on liberal or radical reformers.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c45","front":"Decembrist Revolt","back":"In Russia, the 1825 liberal Decembrist Revolt was utterly crushed by troops loyal to Tsar Nicholas I. Through censorship, military might, secret police, imprisonment, and execution, conservative regimes in central Europe used the powers of the state to repress liberal reform wherever possible.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u5-c46","front":"Censorship","back":"Through censorship, military might, secret police, imprisonment, and execution, conservative regimes in central Europe used the powers of the state to repress liberal reform wherever possible.","sourcePage":24}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-5-f1","prompt":"Seven Years' War"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-5-f2","prompt":"Origins of the French Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-5-f3","prompt":"Liberal phase of the French Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-5-f4","prompt":"Radical phase of the French Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-5-f5","prompt":"Napoleonic Europe"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-6","name":"AP European History - Unit 6","shortName":"Unit 6: Industrialization and Its Effects","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u6-c1","front":"German Confederation","back":"Since the Napoleonic Wars, liberal German reformers had sought to transform absolutist Prussia into a constitutional monarchy, hoping it would lead the German Confederation into a unified nation-state. The agitation that followed the fall of Louis Philippe, on top of several years of crop failure and economic crises, encouraged liberals to press their demands.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c2","front":"enclosure movement","back":"The enclosure movement marked the beginning of the end for the long-used open-farming system, and the rise of capitalist market-oriented estate agriculture and the emergence of a landless rural proletariat.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c3","front":"cottage industry","back":"The cottage industry was a stage of industrial development in which rural workers used hand tools in their homes to manufacture goods on a large scale for sale in a market. Before urbanization and the spread of factories, rural workers would be supplied materials to process by capitalist merchants.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c4","front":"putting-out system","back":"In this putting-out system, these supplier merchants would take a cut of the profit without having to do any labor themselves.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c5","front":"Jethro Tull","back":"Jethro Tull’s seed drill significantly improved the farming process. James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny and Richard Arkwright’s water frame helped the growth of the cotton textile industry.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c6","front":"James Hargreaves","back":"James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny and Richard Arkwright’s water frame helped the growth of the cotton textile industry. James Watt improved upon the steam engine in 1763, and George Stephenson’s Rocket, the first modern steam-powered railroad, revolutionized transportation.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c7","front":"Richard Arkwright","back":"James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny and Richard Arkwright’s water frame helped the growth of the cotton textile industry. James Watt improved upon the steam engine in 1763, and George Stephenson’s Rocket, the first modern steam-powered railroad, revolutionized transportation.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c8","front":"James Watt","back":"James Watt improved upon the steam engine in 1763, and George Stephenson’s Rocket, the first modern steam-powered railroad, revolutionized transportation.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c9","front":"Peterloo Massacre","back":"At the Peterloo Massacre (named in reference to British victory at Waterloo), 18 anti-Corn Law demonstrators were killed. The British parliament then passed the infamous Six Acts, placing controls on a heavily taxed press and eliminated practically all mass meetings.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c10","front":"Six Acts","back":"The British parliament then passed the infamous Six Acts, placing controls on a heavily taxed press and eliminated practically all mass meetings.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c11","front":"Combination Acts","back":"In 1799 Parliament passed the Combination Acts, which outlawed unions and strikes, favoring capitalist business people over skilled artisans. Bitterly resented and widely disregarded by many craft guilds, the acts were repealed by Parliament in 1824.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c12","front":"Robert Owen","back":"Robert Owen advocated for the Factory Act of 1833, which successfully limited the workday of women and child laborers and set minimum hygiene and safety requirements.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c13","front":"Factory Act of 1833","back":"Robert Owen advocated for the Factory Act of 1833, which successfully limited the workday of women and child laborers and set minimum hygiene and safety requirements.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c14","front":"Separate spheres","back":"Separate spheres of work developed; husbands were seen as “wage-earners” and wives as “homemakers.”","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c15","front":"industrial capitalists","back":"A new class of factory owners and industrial capitalists arose, which strengthened the wealth and size of the middle class, which had previously been made up mainly of merchants and professional people. The demands of modern industry regularly brought the interests of the middle-class industrialists into conflict with those of the people who worked for them: the working class.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c16","front":"Charles Darwin","back":"Charles Darwin’s theory of evolution, published in his 1859 work On the Origin of Species by the Means of Natural Selection, states that chance differences among the individual members of a given species that prove useful in the struggle for survival are selected naturally, and they gradually spread to the entire species through reproduction.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c17","front":"theory of evolution","back":"Charles Darwin’s theory of evolution, published in his 1859 work On the Origin of Species by the Means of Natural Selection, states that chance differences among the individual members of a given species that prove useful in the struggle for survival are selected naturally, and they gradually spread to the entire species through reproduction.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c18","front":"Herbert Spencer","back":"English philosopher Herbert Spencer saw the human race as driven forward to ever-greater specialization and progress by a brutal economic struggle that determined the “survival of the fittest.” The poor were the ill-fated weak; the prosperous were the chosen strong.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c19","front":"germ theory","back":"His germ theory and pasteurization significantly advanced medicine in Europe. Pasteur’s early vaccination practice paved the way for the antimalarial drug quinine, which allowed for European imperialism in Africa.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c20","front":"pasteurization","back":"His germ theory and pasteurization significantly advanced medicine in Europe. Pasteur’s early vaccination practice paved the way for the antimalarial drug quinine, which allowed for European imperialism in Africa.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c21","front":"quinine","back":"Pasteur’s early vaccination practice paved the way for the antimalarial drug quinine, which allowed for European imperialism in Africa.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c22","front":"Georg Hegel","back":"Georg Hegel’s nationalistic philosophies deeply influenced his student Karl Marx. In his magnum opus, Das Kapital, published in 1867, Marx applied social-scientific analysis to economic problems, like Adam Smith, though he pushed these liberal ideas in radical directions.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c23","front":"“scientific” socialism","back":"He criticized his socialist predecessors for their fantastical utopian ideals, claiming that his version of “scientific” socialism was realistic. Marx believed, like Hegel, that history moved purposefully in patterns toward an ultimate goal.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c24","front":"heavy industry","back":"Similarly, steel (heavy industry) began to replace textile production (cottage industry) as the dominant product. Automobiles, chemicals, railroads (prevalent), and the telegraph, telephone, and radio set the stage for imperialism and the First World War.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c25","front":"sanitization","back":"Urban workers lived in horrendous conditions, but increased sanitization, advocated by English utilitarian Edwin Chadwick, improved the average worker’s quality of life. Both the urban factory worker and the factory owner classes increased in size.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c26","front":"Edwin Chadwick","back":"Urban workers lived in horrendous conditions, but increased sanitization, advocated by English utilitarian Edwin Chadwick, improved the average worker’s quality of life. Both the urban factory worker and the factory owner classes increased in size.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c27","front":"Max Weber","back":"Max Weber’s sociology cast a bleak light on urban industrial society. While sociologists acknowledged some benefits of rationalization and modernization, they bemoaned the accompanying loss of community and tradition, like Romantics.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c28","front":"Romantics","back":"While sociologists acknowledged some benefits of rationalization and modernization, they bemoaned the accompanying loss of community and tradition, like Romantics.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c29","front":"Bourbon Restoration of 1814","back":"In the Bourbon Restoration of 1814, Louis XVIII granted the Constitutional Charter, a limited liberal constitution. The charter protected economic and social gains made by sections of the middle class and the peasantry in the French Revolution, permitted some intellectual and artistic freedom, and created a parliament with upper and lower houses.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c30","front":"Charles X","back":"Charles X, Louis XVIII’s successor and a reactionary, wanted to establish the old order in France. Increasingly blocked by the opposition of the deputies, Charles’s government turned in 1830 to military adventure in an effort to rally French nationalism and gain popular support.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c31","front":"Louis Philippe","back":"The agitation that followed the fall of Louis Philippe, on top of several years of crop failure and economic crises, encouraged liberals to press their demands.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c32","front":"National Workshops","back":"Moderate republicans, willing to provide only temporary relief, wanted no such thing, and the resulting compromise was to set up National Workshops, which became little more than a vast program of pick-and-shovel public works. This satisfied no one.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c33","front":"June Days","back":"These two clashed in the June Days, and the conflict ended with a constitution favoring a strong executive.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c34","front":"national autonomy","back":"Thus desires for national autonomy within the Austrian Empire enabled the monarchy to maintain power by playing the ethnic groups against each other. The determination of the Austrian aristocracy and the loyalty of its army sealed the triumph of reaction and the defeat of revolution.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c35","front":"universal suffrage","back":"The revolution in the Austrian Empire began in Hungary in March 1848, when nationalistic Hungarians demanded national autonomy, full civil liberties, and universal suffrage. Peasant disturbances broke out in the empire, and the Habsburg emperor Ferdinand I gave in and promised reforms and a liberal constitution.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c36","front":"Friedrich Wilhelm IV","back":"Prussian king Friedrich Wilhelm IV promised to grant Prussia a liberal constitution and to merge Prussia into a new national German state. An elected Prussian Constituent Assembly met in Berlin to write a constitution for the Prussian state, though urban workers wanted much more radical reforms and the Prussian aristocracy wanted much less.","sourcePage":17},{"id":"apeuro-u6-c37","front":"Frankfurt Parliament","back":"He contemptuously refused to accept the “crown from the gutter” offered by the Frankfurt Parliament. By May 1849 all but the most radical deputies had resigned from the parliament, and in June Prussian troops forcibly dissolved what remained of the assembly.","sourcePage":17}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-6-f1","prompt":"Industrial Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-6-f2","prompt":"Industrial capitalism"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-6-f3","prompt":"Urbanization and working conditions"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-6-f4","prompt":"Classical economics"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7","name":"AP European History - Unit 7","shortName":"Unit 7: 19th-Century Perspectives and Political Developments","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u7-c1","front":"Treaty of Paris","back":"The 1856 settlement ending the Crimean War. It weakened Russian influence in the Black Sea region, affirmed the territorial integrity of the Ottoman Empire, and temporarily altered the European balance of power.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c2","front":"liberalism","back":"liberalism: bourgeoisie/middle class, limit Church and state power, laissez-faire capitalism, economic freedom, constitutionalism, civil liberties, privatization, individualism, representative government.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c3","front":"constitutionalism","back":"liberalism: bourgeoisie/middle class, limit Church and state power, laissez-faire capitalism, economic freedom, constitutionalism, civil liberties, privatization, individualism, representative government.","sourcePage":10},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c4","front":"coup d’état","back":"However, Napoleon III illegally dismissed the highly conservative legislature and seized power in a coup d’état. Louis Napoleon restored universal male suffrage and proclaimed the arrival of the Second French Empire.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c5","front":"nationalism","back":"nationalism: national unity based on common language, culture, ethnicity, religion, and shared history.","sourcePage":12},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c6","front":"capitalism","back":"liberalism: bourgeoisie/middle class, limit Church and state power, laissez-faire capitalism, economic freedom, constitutionalism, civil liberties, privatization, individualism, representative government.","sourcePage":13},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c7","front":"survival of the fittest","back":"European nationalism and racism justified these exploits; Social Darwinism, which applied Charles Darwin’s concept of survival of the fittest to race, was popular within imperialists. Rudyard Kipling’s poem The White Man’s Burden describes the “civilizing mission” that Europeans took upon themselves to exploit nonwhite countries under the guise of helping their inhabitants.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c8","front":"Social Darwinism","back":"European nationalism and racism justified these exploits; Social Darwinism, which applied Charles Darwin’s concept of survival of the fittest to race, was popular within imperialists. Rudyard Kipling’s poem The White Man’s Burden describes the “civilizing mission” that Europeans took upon themselves to exploit nonwhite countries under the guise of helping their inhabitants.","sourcePage":14},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c9","front":"Karl Marx","back":"Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels: Early advocates and fathers of socialism. Their piece, the Communist Manifesto, predicted that the proletariat would reach a point of class-consciousness and overthrow the bourgeoisie in a violent class war.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c10","front":"bourgeoisie","back":"liberalism: bourgeoisie/middle class, limit Church and state power, laissez-faire capitalism, economic freedom, constitutionalism, civil liberties, privatization, individualism, representative government.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c11","front":"proletariat","back":"Their piece, the Communist Manifesto, predicted that the proletariat would reach a point of class-consciousness and overthrow the bourgeoisie in a violent class war. This would result in the arrival of communism, a classless, stateless, communal society.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c12","front":"Friedrich Engels","back":"Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels: Early advocates and fathers of socialism. Their piece, the Communist Manifesto, predicted that the proletariat would reach a point of class-consciousness and overthrow the bourgeoisie in a violent class war.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c13","front":"Louis Blanc","back":"Louis Blanc: French socialist who wrote Organization of Work to argue for government-sponsored cooperative workshops. Such workshops would be an alternative to capitalist employment and a decisive step toward a new, noncompetitive social order.","sourcePage":16},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c14","front":"conservatism","back":"A political ideology favoring tradition, established institutions, social hierarchy, and gradual change; after Napoleon, conservatives sought to restore legitimate monarchies and prevent revolutionary upheaval.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c15","front":"Edmund Burke","back":"Edmund Burke: British statesman who derived from the first French Revolution that abstract ideas of “liberty” and “rights” only cause problems; inherited traditions and privileges are bastions of social stability.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c16","front":"Giuseppe Mazzini","back":"Giuseppe Mazzini: Italian author who wrote The Duties of Man, which asserted that language, historic traditions, unification based on “harmony and brotherhood,” and divine purpose defined a national people.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c17","front":"Romanticism","back":"Romanticism: artists/authors/poets, anti-Enlightenment, sociology, nostalgia, conservatism/nationalism, beauty in nature, religion.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c18","front":"Mary Shelley","back":"Mary Shelley: English author and daughter of Mary Wollstonecraft who wrote Frankenstein as a criticism of Scientific and Industrial Revolution advancements that were destroying spiritualism and the natural world. Romantics, like Shelley, revolted against the emphasis on rationality and order that characterized the Enlightenment.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c19","front":"Eugène Delacroix","back":"Eugène Delacroix: French artist who painted Liberty Leading the People in 1831, a romanticized characterization of the French Revolutionary concept of Liberty during the July Revolution.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c20","front":"Adam Smith","back":"Adam Smith: Scottish Enlightenment leader who argued that the guild system, state monopolies, and privileged companies were too restrictive. He preferred free competition, which would protect consumers from price gouging and give all citizens an equal right to do what they did best.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c21","front":"free competition","back":"He preferred free competition, which would protect consumers from price gouging and give all citizens an equal right to do what they did best. He advocated for a “division of labor” that separated craft production into individual tasks to increase efficiency.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c22","front":"invisible hand","back":"His theory of the invisible hand argued that the pursuit of self-interest in a competitive market would be sufficient to improve the living conditions of citizens.","sourcePage":18},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c23","front":"socialism","back":"A family of political and economic ideas seeking greater equality through collective or public control of production, stronger protections for workers, and limits on private concentrations of wealth.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c24","front":"Louis Napoleon","back":"Louis Napoleon (later crowned Napoleon III), nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte, won the presidential election in 1852 after the French Revolution of 1848 and went on to establish a semi-authoritarian regime. Napoleon III’s nationalism was authoritarian, unlike early nationalism, which was liberal or radical.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c25","front":"Napoleon III","back":"Napoleon III believed that rebuilding much of Paris would provide employment, improve living conditions, limit the outbreak of cholera epidemics, and testify to the power and glory of his empire. He hired Baron Georges Haussmann to modernize the city.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c26","front":"French Revolutionof 1848","back":"Louis Napoleon (later crowned Napoleon III), nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte, won the presidential election in 1852 after the French Revolution of 1848 and went on to establish a semi-authoritarian regime. Napoleon III’s nationalism was authoritarian, unlike early nationalism, which was liberal or radical.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c27","front":"semi-authoritarian","back":"Semi-authoritarian nationalism and a new conservatism, based on an alliance of the landed nobles and middle classes, had triumphed in Germany.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c28","front":"universal malesuffrage","back":"Louis Napoleon restored universal male suffrage and proclaimed the arrival of the Second French Empire. Emperor Napoleon III was greatly supported by the public, especially the working class.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c29","front":"Baron Georges Haussmann","back":"He hired Baron Georges Haussmann to modernize the city. A capable city planner, Haussmann bulldozed both buildings and opposition.","sourcePage":19},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c30","front":"Tsar Alexander II","back":"Russia’s military failure informed Tsar Alexander II that Russia needed to undergo industrialization and social change. Alexander abolished serfdom in 1861, though it didn’t help the serfs that much.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c31","front":"zemstvos","back":"His governmental reforms were also halfway measures (such as his zemstvos/local governing councils), but economic industrialization was successful.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c32","front":"Austro-Prussian War","back":"The Austro-Prussian War (Seven Weeks’ War) established Prussia as the dominant German state.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c33","front":"Austro-Hungarian Compromise","back":"After being defeated by Prussia in the 1866 Austro-Prussian War and losing northern Italy, Austria agreed to the Austro-Hungarian Compromise and established the dual-monarchy (Ausgleich) in 1867, which divided the Austrian Empire into two. Each half of the empire from this split onwards dealt with its own ethnic minorities, and the two states, called the Austro-Hungarian Empire (or Austria-Hungary) shared the same monarch and common finance, defense, and foreign affairs ministries.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c34","front":"dual-monarchy","back":"After being defeated by Prussia in the 1866 Austro-Prussian War and losing northern Italy, Austria agreed to the Austro-Hungarian Compromise and established the dual-monarchy (Ausgleich) in 1867, which divided the Austrian Empire into two. Each half of the empire from this split onwards dealt with its own ethnic minorities, and the two states, called the Austro-Hungarian Empire (or Austria-Hungary) shared the same monarch and common finance, defense, and foreign affairs ministries.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c35","front":"Magyarnobility","back":"The Magyar nobility in 1867 Hungary restored the 1848 constitution and used it to dominate the Magyar peasantry and the minority populations until the First World War. Only the top 25% of adult males were given suffrage rights, which made the parliament the mechanism of the Magyar elite.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c36","front":"Congress of Vienna","back":"Klemens von Metternich: Austrian foreign minister that dominated discussion at the Congress of Vienna. He believed that authoritarian, aristocratic government was necessary to protect society from the lesser elements of human behavior, which were easily released in a democratic system.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c37","front":"Sardinia-Piedmont","back":"The struggle for Italian unification under Victor Emmanuel II was supported by Count Camillo Benso di Cavour, a brilliant statesman who served as prime minister of Sardinia-Piedmont from 1852 until 1861. Cavour had realistic unification goals.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c38","front":"Count Camillo Benso di Cavour","back":"The struggle for Italian unification under Victor Emmanuel II was supported by Count Camillo Benso di Cavour, a brilliant statesman who served as prime minister of Sardinia-Piedmont from 1852 until 1861. Cavour had realistic unification goals.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c39","front":"Giuseppe Garibaldi","back":"The super-patriot Giuseppe Garibaldi and his guerrilla band of Red Shirts believed the unification of just the north left the task half done. Cavour controlled Garibaldi and turned popular nationalism in a conservative direction., allowing for Italian unification The kingdom of Italy, established in 1860, was a parliamentary monarchy under Victor Emmanuel II.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c40","front":"Red Shirts","back":"The super-patriot Giuseppe Garibaldi and his guerrilla band of Red Shirts believed the unification of just the north left the task half done. Cavour controlled Garibaldi and turned popular nationalism in a conservative direction., allowing for Italian unification The kingdom of Italy, established in 1860, was a parliamentary monarchy under Victor Emmanuel II.","sourcePage":20},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c41","front":"Kaiser Wilhelm I","back":"Kaiser Wilhelm I employed Otto von Bismarck as Prussian prime minister in 1871. Bismarck was the most successful champion of Realpolitik: politics based on Machiavellian power rather than ideals.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c42","front":"Realpolitik","back":"Bismarck was the most successful champion of Realpolitik: politics based on Machiavellian power rather than ideals. Bismarck’s foreign policy was called “Blood and Iron,” which reflected this idea.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c43","front":"Blood and Iron","back":"Bismarck’s foreign policy was called “Blood and Iron,” which reflected this idea. To prepare to consolidate power, Bismarck reorganized the Prussian army and created a nationalistic conservative constitution for the North German Confederation.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c44","front":"North German Confederation","back":"To prepare to consolidate power, Bismarck reorganized the Prussian army and created a nationalistic conservative constitution for the North German Confederation.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c45","front":"Austro-Prussian War (Seven Weeks’ War)","back":"The Austro-Prussian War (Seven Weeks’ War) established Prussia as the dominant German state.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c46","front":"Ems dispatch","back":"Bismarck goaded French Emperor Napoleon III into war with the Ems dispatch. At the war’s end, Germany was proclaimed as an empire in the Hall of Mirrors in Versailles.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c47","front":"Hall of Mirrors","back":"At the war’s end, Germany was proclaimed as an empire in the Hall of Mirrors in Versailles. This caused the Second French Empire to fall, and France ceded Alsace-Lorraine to Germany and had to pay reparations.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c48","front":"Alsace-Lorraine","back":"This caused the Second French Empire to fall, and France ceded Alsace-Lorraine to Germany and had to pay reparations. France established the relatively stable Third French Republic.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c49","front":"Third French Republic","back":"The republican regime established in France after Napoleon III's defeat in 1870. It endured repeated political crises and lasted until the German invasion of 1940.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c50","front":"Kulturkampf","back":"His Kulturkampf, which tried to put the Catholic Church under governmental control, failed. Bismarck also banned the German Social Democratic Party (SPD) and Socialist political propaganda.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c51","front":"German Social Democratic Party(SPD)","back":"Bismarck also banned the German Social Democratic Party (SPD) and Socialist political propaganda. However, he hoped to avoid a German Socialist revolution by implementing state socialism policies, which included old age pensions, accident insurance, and health insurance.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c52","front":"state socialism","back":"However, he hoped to avoid a German Socialist revolution by implementing state socialism policies, which included old age pensions, accident insurance, and health insurance. This made Bismarck’s Germany the first modern welfare state.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c53","front":"Paris Commune","back":"The Franco-Prussian war forced France to surrender Alsace-Lorraine to Germany in 1871, and the patriotic Parisians proclaimed the Paris Commune, its radical leaders wanting to establish a revolutionary government in Paris and rule without interference from the conservative French countryside.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c54","front":"Adolphe Thiers","back":"Adolphe Thiers, leader of the National Assembly, ordered the French army into Paris and brutally crushed the Commune, killing 20,000 people during the fight.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c55","front":"Dreyfus affair","back":"the Dreyfus affair, a case in which Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish captain in the French army, was falsely accused and convicted of treason, reestablished the conflict. Émile Zola published J’Accuse!, accusing the army of a mistrial and cover-up, and he was sentenced to a year in prison.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c56","front":"Alfred Dreyfus","back":"the Dreyfus affair, a case in which Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish captain in the French army, was falsely accused and convicted of treason, reestablished the conflict. Émile Zola published J’Accuse!, accusing the army of a mistrial and cover-up, and he was sentenced to a year in prison.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c57","front":"Émile Zola","back":"the Dreyfus affair, a case in which Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish captain in the French army, was falsely accused and convicted of treason, reestablished the conflict. Émile Zola published J’Accuse!, accusing the army of a mistrial and cover-up, and he was sentenced to a year in prison.","sourcePage":21},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c58","front":"Berlin Conference","back":"In the 1884 Berlin Conference, 10 major Western powers (including the United States) set standards for Western occupation of Africa. In the late 19th century, these powers nearly completely conquered Africa, leaving only Ethiopia and Liberia independent.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c59","front":"“civilizing mission”","back":"Rudyard Kipling’s poem The White Man’s Burden describes the “civilizing mission” that Europeans took upon themselves to exploit nonwhite countries under the guise of helping their inhabitants.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c60","front":"Leopold II","back":"Dutch King Leopold II and his private army, the Force Publique, terrorized the Congo Free State until 1908, when public outrage over the Congolese oppression reached Europe. Leopold forced the Congolese to harvest African ivory and rubber for Dutch economic gain.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c61","front":"Force Publique","back":"Dutch King Leopold II and his private army, the Force Publique, terrorized the Congo Free State until 1908, when public outrage over the Congolese oppression reached Europe. Leopold forced the Congolese to harvest African ivory and rubber for Dutch economic gain.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c62","front":"Congo Free State","back":"Dutch King Leopold II and his private army, the Force Publique, terrorized the Congo Free State until 1908, when public outrage over the Congolese oppression reached Europe. Leopold forced the Congolese to harvest African ivory and rubber for Dutch economic gain.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c63","front":"Boer War","back":"The Boer War between Afrikaners (Dutch settlers) and the British resulted in British victory, but not after the forcing of Afrikaners and some native Africans into concentration camps.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c64","front":"Rudyard Kipling","back":"Rudyard Kipling’s poem The White Man’s Burden describes the “civilizing mission” that Europeans took upon themselves to exploit nonwhite countries under the guise of helping their inhabitants.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c65","front":"Orientalism","back":"Additionally, the concept of Orientalism was a bit contradictory by nature; Europeans had a fascination with the Oritental stereotype of non-Western peoples, but were also perfectly fine with oppressing them. They believed the West was modern, white, rational, and Christian, while the non-West was primitive, colored, emotional, and pagan or Islamic.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u7-c66","front":"laissez-faire","back":"liberalism: bourgeoisie/middle class, limit Church and state power, laissez-faire capitalism, economic freedom, constitutionalism, civil liberties, privatization, individualism, representative government.","sourcePage":23}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f1","prompt":"Conservatism, liberalism, and socialism"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f2","prompt":"Romanticism and nationalism"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f3","prompt":"Second French Empire"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f4","prompt":"Italian unification"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f5","prompt":"German unification"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f6","prompt":"Austro-Hungarian Empire"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-7-f7","prompt":"New imperialism"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8","name":"AP European History - Unit 8","shortName":"Unit 8: 20th-Century Global Conflicts","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u8-c1","front":"Marxism","back":"Vladimir Lenin and his co-revolutionary Leon Trotsky adapted Marxism to an authoritarian regime. With War Communism, the Leninist regime completely centralized and collectivized business and agriculture.","sourcePage":15},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c2","front":"imperialism","back":"The First World War was caused by militarism, alliances, imperialism, and nationalism.","sourcePage":22},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c3","front":"militarism","back":"The elevation of military power, values, and expansion as central goals of the state. Fascist Italy used militarism to mobilize society and justify aggressive campaigns such as the conquest of Ethiopia.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c4","front":"alliances","back":"The First World War was caused by militarism, alliances, imperialism, and nationalism.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c5","front":"Self-determination on the basis of ethnicity","back":"Self-determination on the basis of ethnicity caused Gavrilo Princip from the Serbian nationalist group the Black Hand to assassinate Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife during their trip to Bosnia. Balkan peninsula nationalists like Princip wanted to liberate Slavic groups from Austro-Hungarian Empire rule and establish their own countries.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c6","front":"Gavrilo Princip","back":"Self-determination on the basis of ethnicity caused Gavrilo Princip from the Serbian nationalist group the Black Hand to assassinate Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife during their trip to Bosnia. Balkan peninsula nationalists like Princip wanted to liberate Slavic groups from Austro-Hungarian Empire rule and establish their own countries.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c7","front":"Black Hand","back":"Self-determination on the basis of ethnicity caused Gavrilo Princip from the Serbian nationalist group the Black Hand to assassinate Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife during their trip to Bosnia. Balkan peninsula nationalists like Princip wanted to liberate Slavic groups from Austro-Hungarian Empire rule and establish their own countries.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c8","front":"Archduke Franz Ferdinand","back":"Self-determination on the basis of ethnicity caused Gavrilo Princip from the Serbian nationalist group the Black Hand to assassinate Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife during their trip to Bosnia. Balkan peninsula nationalists like Princip wanted to liberate Slavic groups from Austro-Hungarian Empire rule and establish their own countries.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c9","front":"Triple Entente","back":"England, France, and Russia formed the Triple Entente in 1907. France, though the long-standing historical enemy of England, sought revenge over its loss in the Franco-Prussian war.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c10","front":"Triple Alliance","back":"After establishing the Triple Alliance with Austria-Hungary and Italy in 1882, German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck believed Germany was a “satisfied” power. Bismarck was the architect of the Alliance System in Europe, however he believed the alliances would lead Europe to peace, not war.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c11","front":"Alliance System","back":"Bismarck was the architect of the Alliance System in Europe, however he believed the alliances would lead Europe to peace, not war. Bismarck was fired by Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1890 because Wilhelm II had greater ambitions for German expansion.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c12","front":"Kaiser Wilhelm II","back":"Bismarck was fired by Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1890 because Wilhelm II had greater ambitions for German expansion.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c13","front":"Schlieffen Plan","back":"The Blitzkrieg was like a successful Schlieffen Plan, taking Poland, Denmark, Norway, Holland, and France. Hitler ruled practically all of continental Europe by July 1940.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c14","front":"trench warfare","back":"This failure on the Western front marked the beginning of trench warfare, in which fighting produced little movement and progress, but massive casualties.","sourcePage":23},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c15","front":"chemical weapons","back":"The chemical weapons, planes, and tanks used in World War I were made possible because of industrial advancements.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c16","front":"total war","back":"At this point in the final stretch of the war, total war policies had been reintroduced for a few years in all participating countries. This was known as War Communism in the Soviet Union.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c17","front":"rationing","back":"In total war, new government ministries mobilized soldiers and armaments and established rationing programs. Censorship offices controlled news about the course of the war, and government planning boards temporarily abandoned free-market capitalism and set mandatory production goals and limits on wages and prices.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c18","front":"Fourteen Points","back":"President Woodrow Wilson created his Fourteen Points, a set of principles which called for open diplomacy, a reduction in armaments, freedom of commerce and trade, national self-determination, and the establishment of the League of Nations.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c19","front":"national self-determination","back":"President Woodrow Wilson created his Fourteen Points, a set of principles which called for open diplomacy, a reduction in armaments, freedom of commerce and trade, national self-determination, and the establishment of the League of Nations.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c20","front":"League of Nations","back":"President Wilson’s idea, the United States never joined the League of Nations, which weakened its ability to accomplish much on a global scale.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c21","front":"1918 Armistice","back":"The 1918 Armistice failed to bring a true end to the fighting. It wasn’t until the 1919 Treaty of Versailles that the war could officially be declared over.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c22","front":"Treaty of Versailles","back":"It wasn’t until the 1919 Treaty of Versailles that the war could officially be declared over.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c23","front":"Peace of Paris","back":"The Treaty of Versailles was a part of a larger series of treaties known as the Peace of Paris. It stripped Germany of its colonies, imposed upon it a massive penalty for warmongering, and forbade Germany from rebuilding its army to larger than 100,000 men.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c24","front":"war guilt clause","back":"It also established the war guilt clause, which officially blamed Germany for the war.","sourcePage":24},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c25","front":"Kellogg-Briand Pact","back":"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, signed in 1928 by Germany, France, and the United States was meant to eliminate the use of war to resolve conflicts. It was unsuccessful; the Second World War started 11 years later.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c26","front":"home front","back":"Women on the home front took up industrial jobs while men were on the front line. A particularly popular employment for women was in munitions factories.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c27","front":"Emmeline Pankhurst","back":"Emmeline Pankhurst: women’s suffrage activist who was key in the United Kingdom’s suffragette movement and helped U.K. women earn the right to vote.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c28","front":"Representation of the People Act","back":"In the English Representation of the People Act (1918), all men and women over 30 could vote. In the Reform Act of 1928, all English women over 21 could vote.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c29","front":"Reform Act of 1928","back":"In the Reform Act of 1928, all English women over 21 could vote.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c30","front":"Russo-Japanese war","back":"The Russo-Japanese war was the first victory of a non-Western power over a European power. This was an embarrassment and financial disaster for Russia.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c31","front":"Bloody Sunday Massacre","back":"Bloody Sunday Massacre: Russian military fired into a crowd in St. Petersburg, resulting in hundreds of casualties and arrests.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c32","front":"October Manifesto","back":"October Manifesto: Tsar Nicholas II authorized a constitution and the creation of the Duma (parliament). However, the tsar still retained executive authority and complete veto power over all legislation.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c33","front":"February Revolution","back":"The February Revolution resulted in Nicholas II’s abdication and the establishment of a Russian provisional republic, which attempted to keep fighting in the First World War.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c34","front":"Kerensky","back":"Kerensky’s provisional government after Tsar Nicholas II’s abdication was weak, and he refused to take Russia out of World War I.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c35","front":"October Revolution(Bolshevik Revolution)","back":"The October Revolution (Bolshevik Revolution) resulted in the Bolshevik takeover of Russia’s provisional government and the eventual establishment of the Soviet Union. Lenin’s government ceded land to Germany in order to exit the First World War in the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c36","front":"Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","back":"Lenin’s government ceded land to Germany in order to exit the First World War in the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c37","front":"Vladimir Lenin","back":"Vladimir Lenin and his co-revolutionary Leon Trotsky adapted Marxism to an authoritarian regime. With War Communism, the Leninist regime completely centralized and collectivized business and agriculture.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c38","front":"Leon Trotsky","back":"Vladimir Lenin and his co-revolutionary Leon Trotsky adapted Marxism to an authoritarian regime. With War Communism, the Leninist regime completely centralized and collectivized business and agriculture.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c39","front":"collectivized","back":"With War Communism, the Leninist regime completely centralized and collectivized business and agriculture.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c40","front":"New Economic Policy (NEP)","back":"This was an economic disaster, and Lenin had to establish the New Economic Policy (NEP), which allowed for petty capitalism and private enterprise in Russia. Similar to Bismarck’s socialist policies, Lenin was willing to dabble in the economic policy he was in opposition to (capitalism; in Bismarck’s case: socialism) in order to preserve his country.","sourcePage":25},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c41","front":"Impressionism","back":"Impressionism blossomed in Paris in the 1870s. Impressionists such as Claude Monet painted neither to glorify the monarchy nor to capture a photo-realistic scene, but to evoke emotion through quick brushstrokes and peaceful colors.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c42","front":"Claude Monet","back":"Impressionists such as Claude Monet painted neither to glorify the monarchy nor to capture a photo-realistic scene, but to evoke emotion through quick brushstrokes and peaceful colors. Notable Monet pieces include Impression, Sunrise; Woman with a Parasol; and Luncheon on the Grass.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c43","front":"Post-Impressionism","back":"Post-Impressionism also utilized color and emotion. Vincent Van Gogh’s Starry Night is probably the most famous post-Impressionist piece.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c44","front":"Vincent Van Gogh","back":"Vincent Van Gogh’s Starry Night is probably the most famous post-Impressionist piece.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c45","front":"Edvard Munch","back":"Edvard Munch’s The Scream is an Expressionist painting. Cubism, like Expressionism, turns the post-World War I feelings of uncertainty, disillusionment, and interest in the subconscious into abstract, geometric art.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c46","front":"Expressionist","back":"Edvard Munch’s The Scream is an Expressionist painting. Cubism, like Expressionism, turns the post-World War I feelings of uncertainty, disillusionment, and interest in the subconscious into abstract, geometric art.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c47","front":"Cubism","back":"Cubism, like Expressionism, turns the post-World War I feelings of uncertainty, disillusionment, and interest in the subconscious into abstract, geometric art. Pablo Picasso was a Spanish Cubist.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c48","front":"Expressionism","back":"Cubism, like Expressionism, turns the post-World War I feelings of uncertainty, disillusionment, and interest in the subconscious into abstract, geometric art. Pablo Picasso was a Spanish Cubist.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c49","front":"Pablo Picasso","back":"Pablo Picasso was a Spanish Cubist. Salvador Dalí’s Surrealist painting The Persistence of Memory depicted melting clocks in an unreal, barren landscape.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c50","front":"Salvador Dalí","back":"Salvador Dalí’s Surrealist painting The Persistence of Memory depicted melting clocks in an unreal, barren landscape. Even more abstract is Dadaism, which challenged the established rules of art through absolutely absurd pieces often containing subliminal political statements.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c51","front":"Surrealist","back":"Salvador Dalí’s Surrealist painting The Persistence of Memory depicted melting clocks in an unreal, barren landscape. Even more abstract is Dadaism, which challenged the established rules of art through absolutely absurd pieces often containing subliminal political statements.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c52","front":"Dadaism","back":"Even more abstract is Dadaism, which challenged the established rules of art through absolutely absurd pieces often containing subliminal political statements.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c53","front":"stream-of-consciousness","back":"The stream-of-consciousness style of writing, championed by Virginia Woolf, William Faulker, and James Joyce, displayed the confusion and uncertainty of the West after the Treaty of Versailles.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c54","front":"Nihilism","back":"Nihilism and existentialism emerged in the post-World War I landscape as well; disappointed after total war, Friedrich Nietzsche attacked Enlightenment philosophy: democracy, nationalism, Christianity, and rationalism. He famously claimed that “God is dead” and was murdered by Christians who no longer truly believed in him.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c55","front":"existentialism","back":"Nihilism and existentialism emerged in the post-World War I landscape as well; disappointed after total war, Friedrich Nietzsche attacked Enlightenment philosophy: democracy, nationalism, Christianity, and rationalism. He famously claimed that “God is dead” and was murdered by Christians who no longer truly believed in him.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c56","front":"Friedrich Nietzsche","back":"Nihilism and existentialism emerged in the post-World War I landscape as well; disappointed after total war, Friedrich Nietzsche attacked Enlightenment philosophy: democracy, nationalism, Christianity, and rationalism. He famously claimed that “God is dead” and was murdered by Christians who no longer truly believed in him.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c57","front":"Franz Kafka","back":"Franz Kafka’s The Metamorphosis exemplifies the disillusionment and shock ordinary people felt in the 1920s.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c58","front":"Sigmund Freud","back":"Sigmund Freud, father of clinical psychiatry, concluded in 1900 that human behavior was basically irrational and governed by the unconscious. His id (animalistic desire), ego (logic), and superego (conscience) attempted to categorize this unconscious.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c59","front":"Albert Einstein","back":"Albert Einstein’s theory of relativity postulated that time and space are relative to the viewpoint of the observer and that only the speed of light is constant for all frames of reference in the universe. This greatly challenged the previously-accepted Newtonian physics.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c60","front":"theory of relativity","back":"Albert Einstein’s theory of relativity postulated that time and space are relative to the viewpoint of the observer and that only the speed of light is constant for all frames of reference in the universe. This greatly challenged the previously-accepted Newtonian physics.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c61","front":"Heisenberg uncertainty principle","back":"Furthermore, the Heisenberg uncertainty principle postulated that nature itself is ultimately unknowable and unpredictable. He suggested that the universe lacked any absolute objective reality and that everything was “relative,” or dependent on the observer’s frame of reference.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c62","front":"Ernest Rutherford","back":"Ernest Rutherford, a leading pioneer of discovery in the science of physics, called the 1920s the “heroic age of physics.” In 1919 he showed that the atom could be split, and by 1944 seven subatomic particles had been identified, the most important being the neutron.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c63","front":"nuclear bomb","back":"This discovery was fundamental to the subsequent development of the nuclear bomb, which the United States used to reign catastrophic destruction upon Japan in World War II.","sourcePage":26},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c64","front":"Léon Blum","back":"French President Léon Blum led France through the Great Depression. His Popular Front made an attempt to deal with the social and economic problems of the 1930s in France.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c65","front":"Popular Front","back":"His Popular Front made an attempt to deal with the social and economic problems of the 1930s in France. Inspired by U.S.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c66","front":"Weimar Republic","back":"So, in the late 1920s liberal democracy seemed to take root in Germany’s Weimar Republic. Elections were held regularly, and the support for republican democracy was growing among the German populace.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c67","front":"Dawes Plan","back":"The 1924 American Dawes Plan was an attempted solution for Germany’s economic crisis. It had the U.S. loaning money to Germany, in which Germany would use to pay reparations to Great Britain and France.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c68","front":"Adolf Hitler","back":"Adolf Hitler absorbed Austria into Greater Germany in 1938. Under the policy of appeasement, British prime minister Neville Chamberlain and the French agreed that Germany should take over the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia, as it contained many ethnic Germans.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c69","front":"Beer Hall Putsch","back":"In a time of political instability, Adolf Hitler, then an obscure politician, proclaimed a “national socialist revolution” in his Beer Hall Putsch (violent attempt to overthrow the government). His attempt was crushed by the emerging republican government, and during his prison sentence, Hitler wrote Mein Kampf, which outlined his plans for future Nazi terror.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c70","front":"Nazi party","back":"However, Germany still had sharp political divisions, and Hitler’s Nazi party attracted support from fanatical anti-Semites, ultranationalists, and disgruntled WWI veterans.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c71","front":"totalitarianism","back":"A system in which a centralized state seeks extensive control over politics, the economy, culture, and private life through dictatorship, propaganda, surveillance, censorship, and repression.","sourcePage":27},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c72","front":"Socialism in one country","back":"Socialism in one country: Stalin intended to strengthen Communism within the Soviet Union, rather than the Marxist and Bolshevik intention for the international spread of communism.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c73","front":"five-year plans","back":"Rapid industrialization: State-supported industrialization of Russia through the implementation of five-year plans (Gosplan) of centrally-planned production.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c74","front":"Gosplan","back":"Rapid industrialization: State-supported industrialization of Russia through the implementation of five-year plans (Gosplan) of centrally-planned production.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c75","front":"Collectivization of agriculture","back":"Collectivization of agriculture: Stalin forced peasants onto collective farms (socialist communal farming collectives). He was resisted by kulaks (land-owning peasants) who he intentionally starved and sent to gulags (Siberian labor camps).","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c76","front":"kulaks","back":"He was resisted by kulaks (land-owning peasants) who he intentionally starved and sent to gulags (Siberian labor camps).","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c77","front":"gulags","back":"He was resisted by kulaks (land-owning peasants) who he intentionally starved and sent to gulags (Siberian labor camps).","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c78","front":"Cult of personality","back":"Cult of personality: Stalin idolized both the deceased Lenin and himself. His status as the leader was proclaimed everywhere, and he and Lenin were seen favorably depicted on propaganda posters.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c79","front":"Leningrad","back":"The name given to St. Petersburg in 1924 after Lenin's death. The city endured a prolonged and devastating German siege during World War II.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c80","front":"Stalingrad","back":"The Soviet city formerly called Tsaritsyn and later renamed Volgograd. The Soviet victory there in 1942-1943 stopped a major German advance and marked a decisive turning point on the Eastern Front.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c81","front":"Political Repression","back":"Political Repression: “Great Purge” in the late 1930s when Stalin purged his political opponents in the military, bureaucracy, and landowning peasants, resulting in around 1 million deaths.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c82","front":"Great Purge","back":"Political Repression: “Great Purge” in the late 1930s when Stalin purged his political opponents in the military, bureaucracy, and landowning peasants, resulting in around 1 million deaths.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c83","front":"Fascism","back":"Fascism: right-wing totalitarianism (total control over citizen life), nationalism, militarism, glorification of the state, hierarchy of classes.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c84","front":"Benito Mussolini","back":"The founder of Italian Fascism and dictator of Italy from 1922 to 1943. He dismantled parliamentary government, built a one-party dictatorship, pursued imperial expansion, and allied Italy with Nazi Germany.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c85","front":"Victor Emmanuel III","back":"Benito Mussolini (r. 1922-1943) seized power from King Victor Emmanuel III during a political crisis.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c86","front":"Corporatist economy","back":"Corporatist economy: Private corporations continue to own the means of production, which are at the command of the government to be used for the common good.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c87","front":"Aggressive nationalism","back":"Aggressive nationalism: The nation is the fundamental unit of organization.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c88","front":"Cult of the Leader","back":"A fascist practice of portraying the dictator as an infallible embodiment of the nation. Mussolini's image as Il Duce and Hitler's Führer cult were reinforced through propaganda, ritual, and mass politics.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c89","front":"Great Depression","back":"The Great Depression damaged Germany for longer than it did the other powers. When it first came about in Germany, Adolf Hitler promised German voters economic as well as political salvation.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c90","front":"National Socialist German Workers (Nazi) Party","back":"On January 30, 1933, Hitler, leader of the National Socialist German Workers (Nazi) Party, the most popular political party in Germany, was appointed chancellor in a coalition government by President Hindenburg. The conservative politicians in power thought they could use Hitler to resolve the political crisis and to clamp down on leftists, controlling him for their benefit.","sourcePage":28},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c91","front":"Enabling Act","back":"Hitler’s Enabling Act, passed through the Reichstag in 1933, gave Hitler dictatorial power for 4 years. The Nazis eliminated their enemies: Communists, Social Democrats, and trade-union leaders.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c92","front":"Nazism","back":"The main components of Nazism were nationalism and anti-Semitism. The Nazis persecuted a number of groups in order to build their Aryan ethnostate, mainly Jews, but also including Slavic peoples, Sinti and Roma, homosexuals, and Jehovah’s Witnesses.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c93","front":"racial hygiene","back":"Nazi bureaucrats inventented more cateogies targeted for “racial hygiene”: the “hereditarily ill” and “asocials.”","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c94","front":"Heinrich Himmler","back":"Hitler’s elite personal guard, the SS, grew rapidly under its leader Heinrich Himmler, and it took over the political police and the concentration camp system.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c95","front":"appeasement","back":"Chamberlain’s policy of appeasement, motivated by the intense pacifism felt after World War I, allowed for Nazi Germany to complete huge conquests uncontested.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c96","front":"Neville Chamberlain","back":"Under the policy of appeasement, British prime minister Neville Chamberlain and the French agreed that Germany should take over the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia, as it contained many ethnic Germans. Soon after, Hitler’s armies invaded and occupied the rest of Czechoslovakia.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c97","front":"Sudetenland","back":"Under the policy of appeasement, British prime minister Neville Chamberlain and the French agreed that Germany should take over the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia, as it contained many ethnic Germans. Soon after, Hitler’s armies invaded and occupied the rest of Czechoslovakia.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c98","front":"Blitzkrieg","back":"Hitler used the Blitzkrieg, or “Lightning War,” strategy with tanks and planes for quick conquest. The Blitzkrieg was like a successful Schlieffen Plan, taking Poland, Denmark, Norway, Holland, and France.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c99","front":"Axis powers","back":"Together, Nazi Germany, imperial Japan, and Fascist Italy formed the Axis powers. Later, Romania, Hungary, and Bulgaria joined the Axis; the Soviet Union, Spain, and Sweden were friendly neutrals.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c100","front":"Battle of Britain","back":"England faced numerous bombings a day in the Battle of Britain, but British Prime Minister Winston Churchill’s order to increase aircraft production forced the Nazis to turn away and refocus their assault on other nations. Additionally, the English development of radar technology and the breaking of the Enigma code gave them a significant advantage in aircraft warfare.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c101","front":"Nazi racial hierarchy","back":"Occupied peoples were treated according to their place in the Nazi racial hierarchy. All were subject to harsh policies dedicated to ethnic cleansing and the plunder of resources for the Nazi war effort.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c102","front":"ghettos","back":"In walled-off ghettos, hundreds of thousands of Polish Jews lived in crowded and unsanitary conditions; over 500,000 people died in these ghettos, the most important being in Warsaw and Lodz. The surviving residents of the ghettos were loaded onto trains and taken to camps.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c103","front":"Auschwitz-Birkenau","back":"In Auschwitz-Birkenau alone, over 1 million people were murdered in gas chambers. According to the New Order, ethnic German peasants were planned to resettle the resulting abandoned lands.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c104","front":"gas chambers","back":"In Auschwitz-Birkenau alone, over 1 million people were murdered in gas chambers. According to the New Order, ethnic German peasants were planned to resettle the resulting abandoned lands.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c105","front":"Grand Alliance","back":"While the Nazis and the Japanese built their empires, Great Britain, the United States, and the Soviet Union joined together in a military pact Churchill termed the Grand Alliance. The United States entered the Second World War in 1941 after imperial Japan preemptively attacked the U.S. military base at Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, destroying the American battleships stationed there.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c106","front":"Atlantic Charter","back":"Roosevelt and British Prime Minister Winston Churchill collaborated on the Atlantic Charter, the prerequisite for the United Nations (which would be formed after the war’s end) in 1914. The Charter went over the Allies’s goals for peace, which included self-determination for nations and economic and social international cooperation.","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c107","front":"Battle of Stalingrad","back":"The Soviet Red Army absolutely decimated the German forces at the Battle of Stalingrad in late 1942; it was the beginning of the end for Germany. In 1943, the larger, better-equipped Soviet forces pushed the German army back along the entire eastern front.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c108","front":"Hiroshima","back":"To end the brutal naval war in the Pacific, American planes dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in Japan on August 6 and 9, 1945. The mass bombing of cities and civilians, one of the terrible new practices of World War II, now ended in the most destructive bombing in human history.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u8-c109","front":"Nagasaki","back":"To end the brutal naval war in the Pacific, American planes dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in Japan on August 6 and 9, 1945. The mass bombing of cities and civilians, one of the terrible new practices of World War II, now ended in the most destructive bombing in human history.","sourcePage":31}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f1","prompt":"Causes of World War I"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f2","prompt":"Alliance systems before World War I"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f3","prompt":"Nationalism in the Balkans"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f4","prompt":"Total war"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f5","prompt":"Home fronts during World War I"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f6","prompt":"Russian Revolution"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f7","prompt":"Bolshevik consolidation of power"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f8","prompt":"Treaty of Versailles"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f9","prompt":"Women's rights after World War I"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f10","prompt":"Great Depression"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-8-f11","prompt":"Weimar Republic"}]},{"id":"apeuro-unit-9","name":"AP European History - Unit 9","shortName":"Unit 9: Cold War and Contemporary Europe","builtIn":true,"sourceTitle":"Historical Summary - AP European History Exam Review","sourceUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i9qEjc3w5DZl7deuECsvaRdvp9fBMxmz/view","cards":[{"id":"apeuro-u9-c1","front":"Stalin","back":"Stalin died in the 1950s and was replaced by Nikita Khrushchev, who privately denounced Stalin’s cult of personality and launched a program of de-Stalinization in the Soviet Union. He laid back the Soviet system of Terror, eventually disbanding the gulags.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c2","front":"Winston Churchill","back":"Winston Churchill proclaimed that an “Iron Curtain” had divided Europe into the East Bloc, which was Communist, and the West Bloc, which was capitalist.","sourcePage":29},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c3","front":"United Nations","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":30},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c4","front":"Marshall Plan","back":"The United States’s Marshall Plan in 1948 was designed to fund the post-WWII “economic miracle” in Europe. Unlike the series of loans that was the post-WWI Dawes Plan, the Marshall Plan just granted money to war-devastated European countries.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c5","front":"economic miracle","back":"The United States’s Marshall Plan in 1948 was designed to fund the post-WWII “economic miracle” in Europe. Unlike the series of loans that was the post-WWI Dawes Plan, the Marshall Plan just granted money to war-devastated European countries.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c6","front":"Iron Curtain","back":"Winston Churchill proclaimed that an “Iron Curtain” had divided Europe into the East Bloc, which was Communist, and the West Bloc, which was capitalist.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c7","front":"East Bloc","back":"Winston Churchill proclaimed that an “Iron Curtain” had divided Europe into the East Bloc, which was Communist, and the West Bloc, which was capitalist.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c8","front":"West Bloc","back":"Winston Churchill proclaimed that an “Iron Curtain” had divided Europe into the East Bloc, which was Communist, and the West Bloc, which was capitalist.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c9","front":"North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)","back":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) was established in 1949. NATO was an anti-Soviet military alliance of Western governments meant to “keep the Russians out, the Americans in, and the Germans down.” Similarly, the Warsaw Pact, a military alliance among the U.S.S.R. and its Communist satellites (including Poland, Czechoslovakia, etc.), was created in 1955.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c10","front":"Warsaw Pact","back":"NATO was an anti-Soviet military alliance of Western governments meant to “keep the Russians out, the Americans in, and the Germans down.” Similarly, the Warsaw Pact, a military alliance among the U.S.S.R. and its Communist satellites (including Poland, Czechoslovakia, etc.), was created in 1955.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c11","front":"Communist satellites","back":"NATO was an anti-Soviet military alliance of Western governments meant to “keep the Russians out, the Americans in, and the Germans down.” Similarly, the Warsaw Pact, a military alliance among the U.S.S.R. and its Communist satellites (including Poland, Czechoslovakia, etc.), was created in 1955.","sourcePage":31},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c12","front":"Cold War","back":"The Cold War is defined by its arms race (including American and Soviet accumulation of nuclear weapons), covert actions (the U.S. establishment of the CIA), and anti-Communist and anti-Western propaganda on both American and Soviet fronts respectively.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c13","front":"arms race","back":"The Cold War is defined by its arms race (including American and Soviet accumulation of nuclear weapons), covert actions (the U.S. establishment of the CIA), and anti-Communist and anti-Western propaganda on both American and Soviet fronts respectively.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c14","front":"nuclear weapons","back":"The Cold War is defined by its arms race (including American and Soviet accumulation of nuclear weapons), covert actions (the U.S. establishment of the CIA), and anti-Communist and anti-Western propaganda on both American and Soviet fronts respectively.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c15","front":"Nikita Khrushchev","back":"Stalin died in the 1950s and was replaced by Nikita Khrushchev, who privately denounced Stalin’s cult of personality and launched a program of de-Stalinization in the Soviet Union. He laid back the Soviet system of Terror, eventually disbanding the gulags.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c16","front":"de-Stalinization","back":"Stalin died in the 1950s and was replaced by Nikita Khrushchev, who privately denounced Stalin’s cult of personality and launched a program of de-Stalinization in the Soviet Union. He laid back the Soviet system of Terror, eventually disbanding the gulags.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c17","front":"Imre Nagy","back":"In October 1956, Hungary elected Imre Nagy, a liberal Communist reformer, as the new prime minister. He never renounced Communism, but he demanded open elections, the relaxation of political repression, and other reforms.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c18","front":"Prague Spring","back":"During the 1958 “Prague Spring,” the long-time Czechoslovak Stalinist leader was voted out of office. Alexander Dubček was his replacement.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c19","front":"Alexander Dubček","back":"Alexander Dubček was his replacement. Dubček was a dedicated Communist, but he believed that he could reconcile genuine socialism with personal freedom and party democracy.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c20","front":"Socialism with a Human Face","back":"The reform program, “Socialism with a Human Face” was enormously popular. Remembering that the Hungarian revolution revealed the difficulty of reforming communism from within, Dubček constantly proclaimed his loyalty to the Soviet Union and the Warsaw Pact.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c21","front":"Berlin Wall","back":"After failing to stop the flow of disgruntled East Germans into the West, the East German authorities built the Berlin Wall between East and West Berlin in 1961. This violated the existing agreements between the Great Powers, but newly-elected U.S.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c22","front":"Cuban missile crisis","back":"When the U.S. discovered missile sites under construction, this caused the Cuban missile crisis. Khrushchev agreed to remove the Soviet missiles in return for American pledges not to disturb the Cuban regime.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c23","front":"Leonid Brezhnev","back":"The reformist Khrushchev was displaced in Leonid Brezhnev’s coup in 1964. Brezhnev began a period of limited re-Stalinization and economic stagnation.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c24","front":"economic stagnation","back":"Brezhnev began a period of limited re-Stalinization and economic stagnation. Soviet leaders launched a massive arms buildup in the mid-1960s all while cautiously avoiding direct confrontation with the United States.","sourcePage":32},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c25","front":"Brezhnev Doctrine","back":"After the 1968 invasion of Czechoslovakia, Soviet premier Leonid Brezhnev announced that the U.S.S.R. would now follow the Brezhnev Doctrine, under which the Soviet Union and its allies had the right to intervene militarily in any East Bloc country whenever they thought doing so was necessary to preserve Communist rule.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c26","front":"Willy Brandt","back":"Chancellor Willy Brandt in West Germany became the first Social Democratic West German chancellor. His Ostpolitik was part of a general relaxation of East-West tensions, called détente, that began in the early 1970s.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c27","front":"Ostpolitik","back":"His Ostpolitik was part of a general relaxation of East-West tensions, called détente, that began in the early 1970s. Though Cold War hostilities continued in the developing world, diplomatic relations between the United States and the Soviet Union grew less strained.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c28","front":"détente","back":"His Ostpolitik was part of a general relaxation of East-West tensions, called détente, that began in the early 1970s. Though Cold War hostilities continued in the developing world, diplomatic relations between the United States and the Soviet Union grew less strained.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c29","front":"Helsinki Accords","back":"The group criticized the government for ignoring the human rights provision of the Helsinki Accords and called on Communist leaders to respect civil and political liberties. They also criticized censorship and argued for improved environmental policies.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c30","front":"Václav Havel","back":"In Czechoslovakia in 1977 a small group of citizens, including future Czechoslovak president Václav Havel signed a manifesto known as Charter 77. The group criticized the government for ignoring the human rights provision of the Helsinki Accords and called on Communist leaders to respect civil and political liberties.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c31","front":"Charter 77","back":"In Czechoslovakia in 1977 a small group of citizens, including future Czechoslovak president Václav Havel signed a manifesto known as Charter 77. The group criticized the government for ignoring the human rights provision of the Helsinki Accords and called on Communist leaders to respect civil and political liberties.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c32","front":"Lech Wałęsa","back":"In Poland, Lech Wałęsa, a Lenin Shipyards electrician and devout Catholic, led workers to organize a free and democratic trade union called Solidarity. As in Czechoslovakia, Solidarity worked cautiously to shape an active civil society.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c33","front":"Solidarity","back":"Poland’s Solidarity inspired other separaratist movements in the Balkan peninsula, and Hungary and Czechoslovakia (Velvet Revolution) drifted toward open elections.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c34","front":"Gdansk Agreement","back":"Solidarity’s leaders pursued a self-limiting revolution, meant only to defend the concessions won in the Gdansk Agreement, which gave workers the right to form trade unions free from state control, freedom of speech, release of political prisoners, and economic reforms.","sourcePage":33},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c35","front":"Velvet Revolution","back":"Poland’s Solidarity inspired other separaratist movements in the Balkan peninsula, and Hungary and Czechoslovakia (Velvet Revolution) drifted toward open elections.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c36","front":"Helmut Kohl","back":"West German chancellor Helmut Kohl presented a 10-point plan for reunification in cooperation with both East Germany and the international community. East Germans immediately fled across the border after the Berlin Wall fell, and their passage marked the beginning of unification.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c37","front":"Gorbachev","back":"After the Communist Party’s country-wide defeats in 1990, Gorbachev ratified a new constitution that formally abolished the Communist Party’s monopoly of political power and expanded the power of the Congress of People’s Deputies. Boris Yeltsin, a radical reform Communist, was elected parliamentary leader of the Russian Soviet Republic in May 1990.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c38","front":"Boris Yeltsin","back":"Boris Yeltsin, a radical reform Communist, was elected parliamentary leader of the Russian Soviet Republic in May 1990. He announced that Russia would put its interests first and declare its independence from the Soviet Union, broadening the base of the anticommunist movement by joining the patriotism of ordinary Russians with the democratic aspirations of big-city intellectuals.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c39","front":"Russian Federation","back":"Locked in a political duel with Gorbachev, Yeltsin declared Russia independent, withdrew from the Soviet Union, and changed the country’s name from the Russian Soviet Republic to the Russian Federation. All the other Soviet republics followed, and the Supreme Soviet dissolved itself on the next day, marking the end of the Soviet Union.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c40","front":"Commonwealth of Independent States","back":"The independent republics of the old Soviet Union then established a loose confederation: the Commonwealth of Independent States.","sourcePage":34},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c41","front":"globalization","back":"After the fall of Communism and the Iron Curtain, European states were able to lead the way to globalization. Globalization is a broad concept, but it contains multinational corporations that use outsourcing to cheap labor markets, allows for the rapid exchange of information through the internet, and through this connects every economy.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c42","front":"multinational corporations","back":"Globalization is a broad concept, but it contains multinational corporations that use outsourcing to cheap labor markets, allows for the rapid exchange of information through the internet, and through this connects every economy. The Western upper middle class benefits from globalization, however it is hard for the working class due to the decrease in industrial factory jobs.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c43","front":"outsourcing","back":"Globalization is a broad concept, but it contains multinational corporations that use outsourcing to cheap labor markets, allows for the rapid exchange of information through the internet, and through this connects every economy. The Western upper middle class benefits from globalization, however it is hard for the working class due to the decrease in industrial factory jobs.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c44","front":"new conservatives","back":"The new conservatives of the 1980s followed neoliberalism because of its roots in the free market and laissez-faire policies. Neoliberal theorists argue that governments should cut support for social services (housing, education, and health insurance), limit business subsidies, and retreat from all regulation.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c45","front":"neoliberalism","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c46","front":"United Nations (UN)","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c47","front":"European Union (EU)","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c48","front":"World Trade Organization (WTO)","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c49","front":"decolonization","back":"It was mainly an instrument of decolonization during the Cold War, but continues to deal with issues such as world hunger and poverty today. The UN also sends troops in attempts to preserve peace between warring parties, as in Yugoslavia in the 1990s.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c50","front":"Maastricht Treaty","back":"The Maastricht Treaty of 1991 formed the standards for the European Union. The EU is meant to enforce peace and unity and promote economic competition with the U.S. and China.","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c51","front":"European Union","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35},{"id":"apeuro-u9-c52","front":"World Trade Organization","back":"Neoliberalism is the dominant philosophy in the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and World Trade Organization (WTO).","sourcePage":35}],"questions":[],"feynmanPrompts":[{"id":"apeuro-unit-9-f1","prompt":"Postwar division of Europe"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-9-f2","prompt":"Cold War alliances"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-9-f3","prompt":"Nuclear arms race"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-9-f4","prompt":"De-Stalinization"},{"id":"apeuro-unit-9-f5","prompt":"Hungarian Revolution"}]}];
